program            = 002_differences_and_comparisons
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Pointer differences and relational comparisons; one-past-end formed and compared but never dereferenced
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
ONE-PAST-END REASONING, stated explicitly because it is what makes this program's
comparison sound. C permits forming a pointer one past the last element of an array, and
permits comparing and subtracting such a pointer; only DEREFERENCING it would be
undefined. This program forms one-past-end pointers for i_buf (i_buf + 10), s_buf, d_buf,
p_buf and for the single object solo (&solo + 1), and uses them ONLY as loop sentinels, as
operands of == != < > >=, and as operands of pointer subtraction. There is no *end, no
(*end), no end-> and no end[...] anywhere in this file. The reverse loop decrements the
pointer BEFORE dereferencing it, so the sentinel value is never read as an object. Two
independently formed one-past-end pointers (end and end_alias) are compared to each other
rather than to themselves, which keeps the comparison non-tautological.
Every subtraction is between two pointers into the same array, so each difference is
representable and defined. The strided loops use < rather than != and their final pointer
is at most one past the end, which is again only compared and subtracted.
No signed integer overflow, no shift out of range, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. The only external symbol is printf; no header is included,
no file, socket, environment variable, clock or random source is touched, and every input
is a literal in this source file.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Pointer differences are element counts, not byte counts, and are cast to long long before
printing, so a 32-bit ptrdiff_t on i686 and a 64-bit ptrdiff_t elsewhere both render
identically.
No address or pointer value is printed. Pointer facts appear only as differences,
comparisons and alignment residues, and every pointer difference is cast to long long
before printing, so the printed text is identical whether ptrdiff_t is 32 or 64 bits.
Plain char is never printed in a signedness-dependent way. The only conversion
specifiers used anywhere in this program are %d, %lld and fixed-precision %.3f, all of
which are target-width independent.
END
